@node Filesystem Design Summary
@section Filesystem Design Summary

VibeOS does not implement a general mount tree.  Instead, it exposes a small
set of fixed backends and a dispatcher:

@itemize @bullet
@item the initramfs backend for the root filesystem, and
@item the ext2/ext3 backend for @file{/usr} and @file{/home}, and
@item an XFS backend for prepared images, including @file{/usr}.
@end itemize

The VFS layer in @file{kernel/src/fs.c} decides which backend owns a path and
then forwards lookup and read operations accordingly.

@node Initramfs Backend
@section Initramfs Backend

The initramfs parser consumes a @code{cpio newc} archive.  It stores a flat
table of entries in memory:

@itemize @bullet
@item normalized path,
@item data pointer,
@item file size, and
@item mode bits.
@end itemize

Important implementation details:

@itemize @bullet
@item Up to 1024 entries are remembered.
@item Archives up to 16 MiB may be copied to a fixed address at
@code{0x30000000}.
@item Paths are normalized to absolute names.
@item Lookups are linear scans over the indexed entry table.
@end itemize

This backend is intentionally simple because the root image is built by the
VibeOS scripts and loaded as an immutable module.

@node ext2/ext3 Backend for /usr and /home
@section ext2/ext3 Backend for /usr and /home

The ext2/ext3 implementation supports @file{/usr} and @file{/home}; the
default @file{/usr} image now uses the XFS backend.  In the shipped boot configuration @file{/usr} is
mounted read-only, while @file{/home} is mounted read-write when a writable SCSI
disk image is attached.  If no writable home disk is available, the kernel falls
back to a volatile in-memory @file{/home} ramdisk.

The @file{/tmp} tree is a separate volatile in-memory ramdisk and is mounted
writable during filesystem initialization.

The kernel backend itself supports both non-journaled ext2 and journaled ext3
filesystems.

It supports:

@itemize @bullet
@item superblock parsing,
@item group descriptor parsing,
@item inode lookup,
@item direct, single-indirect, double-indirect, and triple-indirect data block
resolution,
@item regular file reads,
@item symlink reads,
@item directory scans, and
@item directory child collection for user-visible directory enumeration.
@end itemize

The ext2/ext3 backend claims paths rooted at fixed mount points such as
@file{/usr} and @file{/home}.  Mounted images therefore augment the root
namespace without replacing it.

@node XFS Backend
@section XFS Backend

@file{kernel/src/xfs.c} implements the XFS v4/v5 reader.  The accompanying
@file{xfs_write.inc}, @file{xfs_alloc.inc}, @file{xfs_file.inc},
@file{xfs_dir.inc}, and @file{xfs_mutate.inc} implement writable v5 mounts.
@code{CONFIG_KERNEL_XFS} enables the backend and
@code{CONFIG_KERNEL_XFS_WRITE} enables writes.  Generic mount APIs detect XFS
by its superblock magic.  The default @file{/usr} image remains mounted
read-only; the default @file{/home} image remains ext3.

The reader supports shortform, block, leaf, and node directories; inline and
B+tree extent maps; sparse and unwritten extents; and inline and remote
symlinks.  It checks metadata CRC32c values, identities and bounds.  Inode
numbers remain 64-bit.  Directory enumeration scans data blocks and supports
paging through open directory inodes.

Writable storage mounts support create, overwrite, append, sparse extension,
truncate, mkdir, rmdir, hard links, symlinks, special-file creation, rename
with replacement, chmod, chown, timestamps, and sync.  Allocation updates
maintain both free-space trees, the AG free list, inode trees, extent trees,
and filesystem counters.  Directories transition between shortform, block,
leaf and node layouts.  Open unlinked inodes stay on native AGI orphan lists
until the last descriptor closes; mount recovery reclaims abandoned orphans.

The writer uses native XFS buffer log items and commit records.  New file data
is flushed before allocation metadata commits.  Each log header and payload
is flushed before proceeding to the next record.  Committed metadata is then
written and flushed before a clean marker is published.  Recovery replays
complete buffer transactions, discards incomplete transactions, and covers
torn log records before publishing a clean marker.  Storage write or flush
failure shuts down further access to that mount until remount and recovery.
Storage callbacks must provide a working durability barrier; a write callback
alone is insufficient.

Writable mounts currently require v5 metadata, 4096-byte filesystem and
directory blocks, 512-byte inodes and sectors, an internal unstriped log, and
@code{ftype=1}.  Quotas, finobt, sparse inode chunks, rmapbt, reflink, bigtime,
nrext64, exchange, parent pointers, metadata UUIDs, realtime devices, and
metadata-directory formats are not supported writable.  Unsupported feature
sets are rejected before replay or mutation.  Inodes with attribute forks
are not mutable.  Foreign dirty logs containing
items other than the supported native buffer transaction format must be
recovered on Linux.  Read-only mounts do not replay logs; supply clean images.

To prepare an optional writable image without changing boot defaults:

@example
python3 tools/make_xfs_image.py --writable root-directory output.xfs
@end example

The default builder invocation still creates the read-only @file{/usr} image.
Memory-image and file-backed mount wrappers remain read-only; writable mounts
use @code{fs_mount_storage} and its read, write, and flush callbacks.  XFS
supports four mount points directly below root.  The VFS retains its 128-byte
path and 64-byte component buffers.  Transactions are bounded to 1024 buffers;
operations exceeding that bound fail without publishing metadata.  Directory
indexes currently support one internal node level; allocation and extent tree
rebuilds are limited to 512 nodes.  Large writes commit in
64 KiB portions and can return a short count on failure.  Automatic timestamps
use the kernel's wall clock; explicit timestamps persist.  Read access does
not automatically update atime.  Immutable and append-only inode flags
prevent mutation.

@code{make check-xfs} checks real v4/v5 read-only images and malformed metadata.
@code{make check-xfs-write} checks writable operations, remount persistence,
directory and extent growth, and interrupted writes and recovery, using
@code{xfs_repair -n} to check resulting images.  Host test binaries use static
musl.  @code{make check-xfs-kernel} boots disposable test disks twice with
static musl and dynamic glibc programs, checking writes and persistence.
@code{make check-storage-flush} checks ATA and SCSI barrier behavior.

@node VFS Routing Rules
@section VFS Routing Rules

@findex fs_read
@findex fs_collect_children

The routing rule is intentionally small:

@itemize @bullet
@item If the path belongs to a mounted XFS tree, use the XFS backend with that mount's write policy.
@item If the path belongs to a mounted ext2/ext3 tree such as @file{/usr} or
@file{/home}, use that ext2/ext3 mount.
@item If the path belongs to a ramdisk tree such as @file{/tmp} or the
@file{/home} fallback, use the ramdisk backend.
@item Otherwise use the initramfs backend.
@end itemize

This affects:

@itemize @bullet
@item path lookup,
@item file reads,
@item symlink reads, and
@item child enumeration.
@end itemize

The root directory behaves like a merged view.  It can expose initramfs entries
plus mount points exposed by the ext2/ext3 and XFS backends.

@node Synthetic Directories and Devices
@section Synthetic Directories and Devices

The syscall layer synthesizes directory and device objects beyond what the
underlying backend filesystems contain.

Recognized synthetic directories include:

@example
/
/bin
/dev
/etc
/home
/proc
/sys
/tmp
/usr
/usr/bin
/var
@end example

Recognized synthetic non-directory paths include:

@example
/dev/tty
/dev/console
/dev/null
/dev/fb0
/proc/self/exe
@end example

These special paths are handled in the syscall layer, especially in the helper
functions around @code{path_mode_size}, @code{sys_openat}, @code{sys_ioctl}, and
@code{sys_readlinkat}.

@node Path Resolution and Symlink Semantics
@section Path Resolution and Symlink Semantics

Path resolution follows a predictable set of rules:

@itemize @bullet
@item relative paths are made absolute against either the current working
directory or a directory file descriptor,
@item repeated slashes are collapsed,
@item @samp{.} segments are ignored,
@item @samp{..} segments back up one component when possible, and
@item symlinks are resolved with a fixed maximum depth.
@end itemize

Final-component symlink following is selectable depending on the operation.  For
example, @code{readlinkat} does not follow the final link because it needs the
link object itself.

@node Filesystem Mutability
@section Filesystem Mutability

The shipped root initramfs and @file{/usr} mount are read-only in the current
boot configuration.  The default QEMU run path attaches @file{build/home.ext3}
as a writable SCSI disk and mounts it at @file{/home}.  When that disk is not
present, @file{/home} is still writable but volatile because it is backed by an
in-memory ramdisk.  @file{/tmp} is always writable and volatile.

You can:

@itemize @bullet
@item open files,
@item read files,
@item stat files,
@item traverse directories,
@item read symlinks,
@item access synthetic device-like paths, and
@item create, modify, rename, and remove files under writable @file{/home} and
@file{/tmp}.
@end itemize

The ext2/ext3 backend has read-write support for regular-file data, block and
inode allocation and freeing, truncate, file creation, special-file creation,
directories, symlinks, hard links, unlink, rmdir, rename, chmod, and chown.
Those paths are exposed through VFS helpers and Linux-compatible syscall entry
points, but the shipped @file{/usr} image is still mounted read-only, so
mutation attempts there fail with @code{EROFS}.
